// Everything the prose cannot supply.
//
// The drill cards, the myth deck and the glossary are all generated from the
// markdown at build time, so they cannot drift from what is taught. This file
// holds only what has no source in the prose: a correct answer, a distractor,
// a model answer, and a fault with a cost attached to each way of chasing it.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Self test: model answers, per class
//
// Attempt it, out loud or on paper, then open the answer. These are model
// answers rather than the only correct ones: an answer that differs and is
// defensible with arithmetic and a measurement is worth more than one that
// matches the wording.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

export const selfTest = {
  1: [
    { q: 'A colleague says a 12 V battery bank is safe because it is low voltage. What is right about that, and what is wrong?',
      a: 'Right: 12 V across dry skin cannot push a dangerous current through a person, so it is safe to touch. Wrong: it says nothing about the energy available. That bank can deliver hundreds of amps into a short, weld a spanner to a terminal and start a fire in seconds. Shock risk and energy risk are two separate hazards and only one of them scales with voltage.' },
    { q: 'Why does a cable that is comfortable at 10 A not merely get "a bit warmer" at 20 A?',
      a: 'Because heating is P = I² × R. Doubling the current quadruples the power dissipated in the cable. At 10 A in 0.05 Ω that is 5 W; at 20 A it is 20 W in the same copper, with the same surface area to lose it from. This is why undersized cable does not fail gently and why the squared term is the one to remember.' },
    { q: 'A 16 A circuit has six 500 W lamps on it. Is that acceptable? Show the working.',
      a: 'I = P ÷ V. Six lamps is 3000 W. At 230 V that is 13.0 A, which is under 16 A, so on paper yes. But the 80 per cent continuous limit for a 16 A breaker is 12.8 A, so you are marginally over it, and tungsten inrush is ten to fifteen times running current for the first 50 to 200 ms. It will work and it will nuisance-trip. Five lamps at 10.9 A is the comfortable answer.' },
    { q: 'What is the earth conductor actually for, and why is a missing earth so dangerous?',
      a: 'It gives fault current a path back to the supply so low in resistance that the protective device trips instantly. It is not draining electricity into the soil. A missing earth is dangerous because everything works perfectly until there is a fault, and then the metalwork sits at full mains voltage with nothing tripping, because no extra current is flowing until a person completes the circuit.' },
    { q: 'Why does adding a fixture to a parallel circuit increase the current, while adding one in series makes everything dimmer?',
      a: 'In parallel each branch has the full supply voltage across it and draws its own current, so the total current is the sum and the total resistance falls. In series the same current passes through everything and the supply voltage divides between them, so each item gets less. The parallel case is how a 16 A way ends up carrying 19 A of "small" LED fixtures.' },
  ],
  2: [
    { q: 'Explain, in terms of what the instrument becomes, why a meter in current mode across a supply is dangerous.',
      a: 'In current mode the meter is a near short circuit, a fraction of an ohm, intended to be inserted in series. Placed across a supply it is a deliberate short: the current is limited only by the supply and the meter shunt. That is what the fuse in the meter is for, and it is why an unfused meter is a different product rather than a cheaper one.' },
    { q: 'You measure a divider of two 1 MΩ resistors on 10 V and read 4.0 V rather than 5.0 V. Is the divider faulty?',
      a: 'No. The 10 MΩ meter is in parallel with the lower 1 MΩ arm, giving about 909 kΩ, so the divider genuinely outputs about 4.76 V while you are measuring it and the meter reads close to that. The divider is at 5 V when you are not looking. The rule of thumb: expect a low reading when the source impedance is more than about a hundredth of the meter impedance.' },
    { q: 'Why is "live, dead, live" a procedure rather than a suggestion?',
      a: 'Because every failure mode of a meter — flat battery, broken lead, wrong function selected, blown fuse — produces a reading of zero, which is exactly the reading you were hoping for. Proving the meter on a known live source before and after the test is the only method that distinguishes "the circuit is dead" from "the meter is dead".' },
    { q: 'A cable beeps on continuity. Name two faults it could still have.',
      a: 'A cracked conductor that is touching while the cable lies still and opens on flex, which is the most common cable fault and the one that ruins a technical rehearsal. And a stray strand bridging two pins, which continuity between the same pins will never reveal — that needs the pin-to-pin isolation test. Continuity is one of five tests and the least informative.' },
    { q: 'Why will an averaging meter be wrong on a dimmer output, and by how much?',
      a: 'It measures the mean of the rectified waveform and multiplies by 1.11, which is the correct form factor for a pure sine and wrong for everything else. A phase-controlled dimmer has chopped the waveform, so the mean has changed while the RMS has not. Expect 20 to 40 per cent error, in a direction you cannot predict. A true-RMS meter computes the actual heating value whatever the shape.' },
  ],
  3: [
    { q: 'Why does an LED need a series resistor rather than just the right voltage?',
      a: 'The diode curve is nearly vertical past the forward voltage, so a tenth of a volt is the difference between dark and destroyed and there is no region where it settles at a sensible current. Connected straight across a supply it draws whatever is available, heats, and conducts more at the same voltage as it gets hotter: thermal runaway in seconds. The resistor is the only thing in the circuit setting the current.' },
    { q: 'Calculate the series resistor and its power for a blue LED at 20 mA on a 12 V rail.',
      a: 'A blue LED drops about 3.2 V, so the resistor drops 12 − 3.2 = 8.8 V at 0.02 A. R = 8.8 ÷ 0.02 = 440 Ω, so use 470 Ω from the E12 series, rounding up so the LED is never over-driven. P = I² × R = 0.02² × 470 = 0.19 W, so a quarter-watt part is at 75 per cent of its rating and a half-watt would be more comfortable.' },
    { q: 'What is the flyback problem, and what does it cost to solve?',
      a: 'Interrupting current through a coil makes the collapsing magnetic field generate whatever voltage it needs to keep the current flowing: hundreds of volts from a 12 V supply, in microseconds. It destroys the switching transistor, erodes mechanical contacts, and radiates enough to reset microcontrollers in the same box. A reverse-biased diode across the coil gives that current a loop to circulate in. It costs about two cents.' },
    { q: 'Why is DMX on 5-pin XLR when it only uses three pins?',
      a: 'So that a DMX line cannot be plugged into a microphone input and a microphone cannot be plugged into a dimmer. The two spare pins are the price of making a category error physically impossible. A rig using 3-pin for DMX often works electrically and has discarded that safeguard, and the resulting fault is intermittent and expensive to find.' },
    { q: 'A board has a 100 nF ceramic capacitor next to every chip and a 470 µF electrolytic at the supply entry. Why both?',
      a: 'They do different jobs. The electrolytic is bulk storage, holding the rail up through slow, large current demands. The ceramic is decoupling: it supplies the instantaneous current a chip demands on each clock edge, and it must have low impedance at high frequency. A large electrolytic is poor at high frequency because of its own internal inductance, which is why a big one alone does not do the ceramic’s job.' },
  ],
  4: [
    { q: 'Name the three things a solder joint needs at once, and say which one each common fault is a failure of.',
      a: 'Clean metal, enough heat in the right place, and a short time. A dry joint is a cleanliness failure: oxide, no flux. A cold joint is a heat failure, or movement while cooling. An overheated joint with lifted pads and shrunk insulation is a time failure. Naming which of the three failed is what turns soldering from a knack into a skill.' },
    { q: 'Why does a cable fail at the connector rather than in the middle?',
      a: 'Because that is where the flexing is concentrated. The copper work-hardens at one point, a strand breaks, the remaining strands carry the same current in less metal and run hotter, more break, resistance rises. The connection becomes intermittent long before it becomes open, and that intermittent phase is where the money is lost. The clamp must grip the outer jacket so the strain never reaches the joints.' },
    { q: 'A network cable passes a continuity test on all eight pins and drops packets at gigabit over 50 m. What is the most likely fault?',
      a: 'Split pairs: pins 3 and 6 are one twisted pair, and if they have been run in two different pairs the cable still connects every pin to the right pin. The twist is the entire mechanism by which a pair rejects interference, so a split pair links happily and fails under load over distance. A cheap tester that only checks continuity calls that cable good.' },
    { q: 'Why is heatshrink not strain relief?',
      a: 'Heatshrink is insulation with a little mechanical support. It does not grip the outer jacket, so flexing still reaches the joint. A joint whose only support is heatshrink will move, and solder is brittle: it cracks. The strain relief is the clamp on the jacket, and everything else is insulation.' },
    { q: 'Give the XLR pinout for audio and for DMX, and say what to do before you strip anything.',
      a: 'Audio 3-pin: pin 1 screen, pin 2 hot, pin 3 cold. DMX 5-pin: pin 1 screen, pin 2 data minus, pin 3 data plus, pins 4 and 5 unused in almost every rig. Before stripping: put the shell and the boot onto the cable, the right way round. Everybody forgets this once, and unsoldering three joints to slide a boot on teaches it better than being told.' },
  ],
  5: [
    { q: 'A relay is marked "10 A 250 VAC". Why can it not switch 10 A of a 24 V DC motor?',
      a: 'Two derating factors apply together. The load is inductive, so the collapsing field sustains an arc on break, which alone derates it to about a third. And it is DC, so there is no zero crossing to extinguish that arc: once struck it burns until the gap grows enough. A relay rated 10 A at 250 V AC may be rated half an amp at 30 V DC, and the small print says so.' },
    { q: 'Why is a MOSFET driven at 5 V from a 3.3 V-tolerant standard part the worst case rather than simply a weaker one?',
      a: 'Because half on is the worst possible state. Fully on it has almost no voltage across it; fully off it has almost no current through it; power is the product, so both are nearly free. Partly on it has substantial voltage AND substantial current simultaneously, and R_DS(on) may be ohms rather than milliohms. At 5 A that is tens or hundreds of watts, and the part is destroyed in about a second.' },
    { q: 'How is optical isolation defeated without touching the optocoupler?',
      a: 'By connecting the two grounds. The component is still doing its job perfectly and there is no conductive path across the package, but there is now a path around the outside. Isolation is a property of the whole system rather than of one part, and a shared ground added "for convenience", or a mounting screw touching both planes, removes it entirely.' },
    { q: 'Forty LED fixtures on one switch trip the breaker at switch-on but run at 8 A. Explain, and give the engineering answer.',
      a: 'Each fixture has an input capacitor that looks like a short circuit for the first few milliseconds while it charges: 20 to 100 A per unit. One is nothing; forty energised at the same instant is a very large momentary current and the breaker responds to it correctly. The answer is sequencing, bringing circuits up in groups about a second apart so the peaks never coincide, rather than a bigger breaker, which just means a fault has to get bigger before anything notices.' },
    { q: 'Why is a 10 A solid state relay not a drop-in replacement for a 10 A mechanical relay?',
      a: 'It drops about 1 to 1.5 V while conducting, so at 10 A it turns 10 to 15 W into heat continuously and needs a heatsink. A mechanical relay has near-zero contact resistance and dissipates almost nothing while closed. The SSR wins on silence, speed and wear; it loses on dissipation, and the most common SSR failure is thermal, caused by mounting it on nothing.' },
  ],
  6: [
    { q: 'Which single omitted component would make your driver board dangerous rather than merely broken, and why?',
      a: 'The gate pull-down. Without it, a MOSFET gate is a floating high-impedance node with charge stored on its capacitance, so the channel can sit partly on indefinitely with no signal present. The board would appear to work while the control cable was connected and do something undefined the moment it was unplugged with the load powered. The input pull-down is the same argument at the safe end.' },
    { q: 'Why is the control-ground to load-ground resistance the most important measurement on the board?',
      a: 'Because it is the only test of the thing the board exists to provide. Every other measurement checks that a channel switches, which many designs do. That one measurement checks that a fault on the load side cannot reach whatever a person is touching, and a single stray wire, a mounting screw across both planes, or a copper pour that was not split will read as a short while the optocouplers are all working perfectly.' },
    { q: 'Why test one channel completely before populating the other three?',
      a: 'Because a systematic mistake, a reversed part, a wrong resistor value, a misread footprint, is then found once instead of four times. Building all four and testing at the end means you repeat every error before discovering any of them. Every experienced builder does this and every beginner thinks it is slower; it is slower exactly once, on the board where you made no mistakes.' },
    { q: 'Your board’s documentation must state the behaviour on loss of control signal. What is yours, and why does it matter more than the ratings?',
      a: 'Outputs off, because both pull-downs force a definite off state when nothing is driving the inputs. It matters more than the ratings because it is the sentence a production manager actually reads: it answers "what happens if somebody kicks the cable out during the show". Ratings can be looked up from the components; failure behaviour cannot be looked up from anything.' },
    { q: 'The dead test across the supply terminals reads 15 Ω. What do you do, and what would happen if you did not?',
      a: 'Do not power it. Find the short: a solder bridge, a reversed electrolytic, a part in backwards. Powering it turns a five minute inspection into a destroyed board and possibly a destroyed supply, and the damage will hide the original fault. The dead tests exist entirely so that the first fault you find is found at zero energy.' },
  ],
  7: [
    { q: 'Why do modern audio systems bridge impedances rather than match them?',
      a: 'Matching maximises power transfer and costs you exactly half the signal voltage. Information in audio is carried by voltage, so you want voltage transfer: a load at least ten times the source impedance, which delivers over 90 per cent of the available voltage. Matching survives as folklore from the era of long telephone lines and transformers. The genuine exceptions are loudspeakers and radio-frequency transmission lines, which includes DMX and network cabling.' },
    { q: 'Two working boxes hum when connected. Explain the mechanism as a circuit.',
      a: 'Both chassis are bonded to protective earth, and those two earth paths meet a long way away at the distribution board. Real conductors have resistance and other equipment draws current through them, so the two chassis sit a few millivolts apart at 50 Hz. Connecting them with a signal cable whose screen joins them completes a loop; a small voltage around a low-resistance loop drives a current, and that current through the screen’s resistance appears in series with the signal.' },
    { q: 'Why can you lift the screen at one end of a balanced cable but not the earth pin of a mains plug?',
      a: 'In a balanced line the screen carries no signal, only shielding, so disconnecting it at the receiving end breaks the loop and keeps the audio. The mains earth is the fault path that makes a protective device trip when a live conductor touches a chassis. Lifting it also cures the hum, and it removes the thing keeping somebody alive. They are opposite actions, not variations of one technique.' },
    { q: 'You hear 100 Hz rather than 50 Hz. What does that tell you and where do you look?',
      a: 'That it is rectifier ripple rather than induction or a ground loop: twice the mains frequency means a power supply. Look at the smoothing capacitor, which is the most common failure in old show equipment and is often visibly domed or crusted. Being able to say that in the first minute is the difference between a ten minute fix and an evening.' },
    { q: 'Why does a long unbalanced instrument cable sound dull, and what fixes it?',
      a: 'Cable capacitance, roughly 100 pF per metre, forms a low-pass filter with the source impedance. A guitar pickup at tens of kilohms over 20 m puts the corner inside the audio band and the top end is lost. A DI box at the instrument fixes it: it presents about 1 MΩ so the pickup is not loaded, and hands on a low-impedance balanced signal for which the same cable capacitance is irrelevant.' },
  ],
  8: [
    { q: 'A trace will not stand still. What is almost always wrong, and what are the two things to check?',
      a: 'The trigger, not the timebase. Check that the trigger source is the channel actually carrying the signal, and that the level is inside the signal’s amplitude — a level above the peak means nothing ever crosses it and the scope never starts a sweep, which looks identical to no trigger at all. The instinct is to fiddle with the timebase and it is almost never the problem.' },
    { q: 'A 12 V rail is suspected of ripple. Why does DC coupling at 5 V per division not show it?',
      a: 'Because the 12 V offset uses most of the screen at that sensitivity, and 40 mV of ripple is a hundredth of one division. AC coupling removes the DC so you can wind the vertical sensitivity to 10 or 20 mV per division and the ripple fills the screen. Choosing the coupling wrongly means the fault is on the screen and you cannot see it.' },
    { q: 'Why can you not put a standard scope probe on a live mains conductor?',
      a: 'The probe’s ground clip is connected to the scope chassis, which is connected to mains earth. Clipping it to a live conductor creates a short circuit through the instrument. Mains measurements need a differential probe or an isolation transformer, and the instrument you are protecting is secondary to the person holding it.' },
    { q: 'Give the five characterising questions for a hum problem and say what the second one buys you.',
      a: 'Hum or buzz? Does it change with the channel fader? Is it there with the input unplugged? Does touching the connector shell change it? Does it change with the lighting state? The fader question splits the system in half in one step: if the hum changes with the fader it enters before that fader, and if it does not it enters after. Five questions, ninety seconds, no tools.' },
    { q: 'Why is proving the fix a separate step rather than the end of the previous one?',
      a: 'Because if you cannot make the fault come back by undoing your change, you found a coincidence rather than a cause. Systems are full of things that change at the same time as something else. This step is skipped almost universally and it is the one that separates a repair from a hope; unproven fixes reappear during performances, usually when the person who made the change is not in the building.' },
  ],
  9: [
    { q: 'What does "DMX has no addressing" mean in practice?',
      a: 'The protocol broadcasts 512 bytes and every device receives all of them, picking out the ones starting at the address you set on it. So two fixtures on one address both respond identically forever and nothing anywhere reports an error, because there is no addressing to collide. It is also one direction only: a fixture cannot tell the console anything, so everything you know about the state of the rig you know because you sent it.' },
    { q: 'Explain what a terminator does, in terms of impedance.',
      a: 'A cable has a characteristic impedance, 110 Ω for DMX cable, set by its physical construction. A signal meeting an impedance discontinuity — an open end — reflects, and that reflection travels back and adds to the following bits. A 120 Ω resistor at the far end matches the cable and absorbs the energy instead of reflecting it. Without one the reflection is present and merely small enough to survive today.' },
    { q: 'Calculate the DMX refresh rate for a full universe, and say why it changes with channel count.',
      a: 'Each slot is 44 µs with its start and stop bits. A full packet is the break (92 µs minimum) plus mark after break (12 µs) plus 513 slots including the start code: 92 + 12 + 513 × 44 ≈ 22,676 µs, so about 44 Hz. Sending fewer slots shortens the packet proportionally, so 128 channels refreshes at roughly 160 Hz. That is a real and visible effect on fast moving-light chases.' },
    { q: 'Why do event protocols need more reliability than state protocols, not less?',
      a: 'A state protocol like DMX sends the complete current picture forty-four times a second, so a lost packet is corrected by the next one and the failure mode is flicker. An event protocol like MIDI Show Control says a thing happened, once. If that is lost, nothing corrects it: the show is now in the wrong state until somebody intervenes. People habitually assume the opposite, because events feel less demanding.' },
    { q: 'Why has sACN largely replaced Art-Net on large rigs?',
      a: 'Multicast against broadcast. Art-Net commonly broadcasts, so every device on the network receives and processes every universe and discards what it does not need, and the processing lands on the node, usually the weakest device present. sACN uses multicast, so a device subscribes to the universes it needs and the switch forwards only those. On a thirty-two universe rig that is the difference between a network that works and one that saturates.' },
  ],
  10: [
    { q: 'A fixture in the middle of a run flickers intermittently. Give the first three things you would check and why in that order.',
      a: 'First: is there a terminator at the far end, because an unterminated line produces exactly this symptom at a position unrelated to the fault. Second: the topology, because a passive star produces the same symptom and changes as you touch things. Third: the cable type, because microphone cable at 50 Ω where 110 Ω belongs behaves the same way at length. All three are boundary questions answerable by looking, before any instrument comes out.' },
    { q: 'Why does an open screen produce a fault that only appears during certain scenes?',
      a: 'With no shield, the data pair is exposed to induced interference, and the largest source in a theatre is phase-control dimmer harmonics. The fault is dormant until the dimmers come up. If the notes say "it only happens during the ballroom scene", that is diagnostic information rather than a complaint: it tells you the trigger is in another department and points at cable routing and screening.' },
    { q: 'What makes a passive DMX Y-splitter so much worse than no splitter at all?',
      a: 'It creates multiple unmatched branches and therefore multiple reflection paths, whose constructive and destructive interference depends on the exact lengths of each branch. Some branches work, others do not, and which ones changes when you touch things or repatch. There is no single faulty component and no test on any individual cable finds it. An active splitter buffers and independently terminates each output.' },
    { q: 'A node’s display looks completely healthy and no fixtures respond. What is the most likely cause?',
      a: 'The console and the node are on different subnets, so they cannot reach each other while both devices report themselves as fine. Read both IP addresses rather than assuming. This wastes more time than any other network fault precisely because nothing is broken and nothing reports an error; the first three network checks are link, address, then traffic, in that order.' },
    { q: 'Why is the rig drawing the deliverable rather than the rig?',
      a: 'Because the drawing is what makes fault-finding fast, and fault-finding is where the hours actually go. A team with a correct drawing finds a wrong-universe fault in ninety seconds; a team without one starts unplugging things. The test of the drawing and the labelling together: could somebody who has never seen this rig find the third fixture on run two, in the dark, in ninety seconds?' },
  ],
  11: [
    { q: 'What does delay(1000) actually do, and why does it matter in a prop rather than on a bench?',
      a: 'It sits in a tight loop counting for one second while the processor does nothing else at all: no buttons read, no DMX received, no emergency stop noticed. On a bench that is invisible. In a prop it means up to a second of complete deafness, and the operator’s experience is "the button did not work sometimes". The replacement is to check millis() and decide, so the loop runs thousands of times a second.' },
    { q: 'Why is now − last >= INTERVAL correct where now >= last + INTERVAL is not?',
      a: 'The millisecond counter overflows after about 49 days. Unsigned subtraction handles the wrap correctly because the difference is still right modulo the counter width; addition overflows and the comparison fails. A prop in a long-running installation will meet this exactly once, about seven weeks in, and the bug is essentially undiagnosable if you do not know it exists.' },
    { q: 'An input pin with nothing connected reads randomly and changes when you move your hand. Explain and give the fix.',
      a: 'It is not noise in the reading; the pin genuinely sits at whatever voltage the surrounding electric field puts on its tiny capacitance, because nothing is driving it. A floating input is undefined, not off. The fix is a 10 kΩ pull-up or pull-down defining the idle state. In show hardware prefer the arrangement where the safe state is the de-energised one, so a cut cable reads as not pressed.' },
    { q: 'Why is a 12-bit ADC not automatically more accurate than a 10-bit one?',
      a: 'Resolution is not accuracy. The reading is a comparison against the reference, so if the reference is the 5 V USB rail and it sags to 4.7 V when a motor starts, every reading moves by six per cent while nothing physical has changed. Source impedance matters too: above about 10 kΩ the sampling capacitor does not finish charging and the reading is low. Both dominate long before bit depth does.' },
    { q: 'What decides the PWM frequency for a stage lamp, and what goes wrong at each end?',
      a: 'Below about 100 Hz it flickers visibly, worse in peripheral vision and far worse on camera. Between a few hundred hertz and 20 kHz, motors and inductors whine audibly, which matters in a quiet auditorium. Above 20 kHz it is inaudible and invisible but switching losses rise and gate drive matters more. If the show is being filmed, the camera is the constraint that actually decides it.' },
  ],
  12: [
    { q: 'Why must a prop initialise to a safe state rather than resuming its previous one?',
      a: 'Because power blips happen, and a prop that resumes its last state will fire during a scene it is not in, most likely in the interval when nobody is watching for it. Initialise the state variable to SAFE explicitly rather than relying on whatever the compiler left there, and put the power-up behaviour on the documentation sheet, because it is the behaviour an audience will eventually see.' },
    { q: 'A sensor reports a distance of −4 mm. Why is filtering that reading the wrong response?',
      a: 'An impossible reading means the sensor or its wiring has failed, not that the world has become strange. Filtering it hides a fault and lets the prop act on invented data, which is how a device does something violent because a wire came loose. Detect it, name it, and go to a safe state. An impossible value is information, and the information is that something is broken.' },
    { q: 'Give three defensible answers to "what happens when the cue arrives during FLARE", and say why the question matters.',
      a: 'Ignore it, restart the flare, or queue it for after SETTLE. All three are correct in different productions and the choice must be deliberate and documented. It matters because an operator unsure whether the first press registered will press again, every time, on every production, forever. The double tap is normal operation rather than an edge case.' },
    { q: 'What does a watchdog timer give you, and what does it not?',
      a: 'It resets the chip within a second or two if your code hangs, recovering from a software lock-up nobody can otherwise clear. It does nothing about wrong logic, and a prop that resets every two seconds into a bad state is worse than one that stopped, because it keeps acting. It has to be combined with a defined power-up state or it makes things worse rather than better.' },
    { q: 'Why does a prop need a status indicator that blinks a pattern rather than one that says "powered"?',
      a: 'Because the person in the building when it fails has never seen your code and does not have a laptop. A pattern per state — one blink idle, two running, rapid for a fault — lets them diagnose from six metres away with only the documentation sheet. "Powered" tells them something they can already see from the fact that nothing else in the rack is dark.' },
  ],
  13: [
    { q: 'Why is a safety-related contact wired normally closed?',
      a: 'So that a cut cable, a corroded contact or a pulled connector produces the unsafe reading and the machine stops. Wired normally open, all of those read as "not pressed", which means safe, forever, and nothing anywhere reports a fault: the guard that was designed to stop the machine has silently stopped existing. It is the most examinable idea in the class for exactly that reason.' },
    { q: 'How do you choose the fixed resistor for a sensor in a divider, and why that rule?',
      a: 'Approximately the geometric mean of the sensor’s range, so for an LDR running 1 kΩ bright to 100 kΩ dark, about 10 kΩ. That places the useful part of the curve in the middle of the ADC’s span rather than squashed at one end. Then check that the output impedance, R1 in parallel with R2, stays under about 10 kΩ so the ADC can charge its sampling capacitor.' },
    { q: 'A stepper has been commanded to move 200 steps and the mechanism jammed at step 40. What does the system believe?',
      a: 'That it moved 200 steps. Nothing in a stepper reports lost steps, which is precisely the trade for getting precise positioning without a feedback sensor. Every stepper system in a show needs a home switch and something that verifies position at least once per cycle, or it drifts over a run and is discovered on the night it matters.' },
    { q: 'Explain peak and hold and say when you would insist on it.',
      a: 'Drive a solenoid at full power for the 100 ms or so it takes to pull in, then reduce to a lower PWM duty to hold, because once the magnetic circuit is closed far less force is needed to keep it there. Insist on it whenever a designer wants a latch or catch held for a scene or an act: many solenoids are rated for a duty cycle rather than continuous use and will reach burn temperature and then fail.' },
    { q: 'In a chain that takes two seconds to move, why is reducing the debounce from 50 ms to 5 ms usually the wrong optimisation?',
      a: 'Because the mechanical time dominates by more than an order of magnitude and nobody in the audience can perceive 45 ms on a two second movement. Meanwhile a 5 ms debounce lets contact bounce through, producing double triggers. The effort belongs on the parts that are actually visible; know whether you are building a snap or a movement before you spend a day shaving milliseconds.' },
  ],
  14: [
    { q: 'Why test the logic on a lamp before connecting the real actuator?',
      a: 'Because a logic error that lights a lamp is a lesson and the same error firing a solenoid or a motor near a hand is an incident report. It also isolates the variables: if the effect misbehaves once the actuator is connected, you already know the state machine is right and the problem is in the drive or the mechanism.' },
    { q: 'Describe the hundred cycle test and name three things it finds that a demonstration does not.',
      a: 'Run the effect a hundred times automatically and log the first, fiftieth and hundredth of everything that could drift. It finds solenoid heating, stepper position drift and contact or connector wear, all of which appear between fifty and several hundred cycles. A show run lands squarely in that range, and a demonstration is one operation with everybody watching.' },
    { q: 'What is the stranger test and why is it the assessed part?',
      a: 'Somebody who has not seen the device, given only your documentation sheet and no conversation, must power it up, trigger it, cause a fault, identify the fault from the indicator, and reset it. It is assessed because the sheet is what goes on the production and you will not be in the building. Anything they had to guess is a defect in the sheet rather than a shortcoming in them.' },
    { q: 'Compare the three trigger patterns and say which you would reach for first.',
      a: 'Cue arms and sensor fires: the desk controls whether, the sensor controls when. Either one fires it: simple, and a false sensor trigger fires the effect in the wrong scene. Both within a window: robust against both, and it fails silently when they do not coincide, so it needs an indicator showing you missed. Reach for the first, because it gives each side the decision it is actually good at.' },
    { q: '"It fails safe, so a failure does not matter." What is wrong with that?',
      a: 'Failing safe means nobody is hurt. It still means the effect did not happen, and a production needs to know how likely that is and what the indicator will show when it does. Safe and reliable are separate requirements, and a device that is safe and unreliable will be removed from the show, which is the correct outcome but not the one you wanted.' },
  ],
  15: [
    { q: 'Why does no fuse or breaker protect a person?',
      a: 'A 13 A fuse permits 13 amps to flow indefinitely, and a lethal current is around 50 milliamps: about four hundred times less. Fuses and breakers exist to protect the cable from overheating and catching fire, which is a real and different job. Only an RCD, isolation or double insulation protects a person, and each of them has a specific gap.' },
    { q: 'Explain how an RCD detects a fault, and name two things it will not save you from.',
      a: 'It compares the current flowing out in the live conductor with the current returning in the neutral. In a healthy circuit they are identical; if some is returning by another route — through a person, through damp insulation — the two no longer balance and it opens the circuit at about 30 mA in under 30 ms. It will not save you from touching live and neutral together, because the currents still balance, and it does not respond to overload at all.' },
    { q: 'A machine has one big red latching button that removes power. Which properties of an emergency stop does it still lack?',
      a: 'Dual channel and monitoring, most likely. A single contact can weld closed and nobody would know until the day it was needed. Two independent contacts in two independent circuits, watched by a safety relay for disagreement, turn an undetectable failure into a machine that will not start. Check also that releasing the button does not restart anything, and that it acts on the power source rather than asking software.' },
    { q: 'Why does isolation need your own lock rather than telling a colleague?',
      a: 'Because people go home and shifts change, and a circuit switched off for no visible reason gets switched back on. "I told him I was working on it" has killed people. One lock per person working, key on the person rather than in a drawer, a tag saying who and how to reach them, and the isolation cannot be restored until every lock is removed.' },
    { q: 'Why does half-splitting beat working along the chain, and by how much on a sixteen-point system?',
      a: 'Because each test eliminates half the remaining possibilities rather than one. Sixteen points is four tests by half-splitting and up to sixteen along the chain, averaging eight. The gap widens as the system gets bigger, which is exactly when you are most tempted to guess. Under time pressure methodical is faster than clever, and this is where you find that out.' },
  ],
  16: [
    { q: 'Why is the fault-finding exam worth more than the build?',
      a: 'Because it is the closest thing in the course to the job. You will spend your career diagnosing systems you did not build, under time pressure, with somebody asking when it will be fixed. The build proves you can make one thing; the exam proves you have a method that works on a device you have never seen, and only one of those two transfers.' },
    { q: 'How can you score well on a fault you did not fix?',
      a: 'By establishing the boundary before touching anything, half-splitting within it, changing one thing at a time with a written log, and closing with what you excluded and what your next test would be. That is a professional handover at the end of a shift. A lucky find with no log scores below it, because luck does not transfer to the next fault and a method does.' },
    { q: 'Your device has a limitation you cannot design out in the time available. What do you do?',
      a: 'Write it on the sheet, specifically. A limitation you have named is engineering; the same limitation undocumented is a defect. Sheets that list three specific known limitations score above sheets that list none, and a production manager who reads "the load sits at supply potential when off" can plan around it. One that discovers it is a different conversation.' },
    { q: 'What is the correct response to finding a safety issue during the exam?',
      a: 'Stop, isolate, report. That is the full-mark answer regardless of how much time is left or how close you were to something else, and continuing to work on it is a zero. It is not part of the puzzle. The same rule applies on a production, where the pressure to carry on is considerably greater than it is in an exam room.' },
    { q: 'What has this course actually qualified you to do?',
      a: 'Not mains work: that is regulated and jurisdiction-specific, and no academic course grants it. It has qualified you to read a schematic, measure a claim rather than accepting it, specify a system so a supplier takes it seriously, spot a defeated interlock, run a diagnostic process on equipment you have never seen, and build low-voltage show electronics to a standard that can go on a production.' },
  ],
};

